4.0 Configure Browser with Certificate
This step is necessary for recording HTTPS traffic using JMeter. When JMeter acts as a proxy, it generates a temporary certificate that must be trusted by your browser to avoid security warnings.
4.1 Start the Recorder
In the JMeter GUI, go to the HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder element and click the Start button.
4.2 Install the Certificate
If you are recording a secure (HTTPS) website, JMeter will automatically generate a temporary root certificate:
ApacheJMeterTemporaryRootCA.crt (located in the JMeter bin/ directory).
This certificate must be installed in your browser to avoid "untrusted connection" warnings.
4.3 Import the Certificate in Firefox
Open Firefox and go to Settings → Privacy & Security
Scroll down to Certificates and click View Certificates
In the Certificate Manager, go to the Authorities tab
Click Import… and select the JMeter certificate (ApacheJMeterTemporaryRootCA.crt)
Accept the trust prompt and click OK
📸 [Screenshots showing the Certificate Manager and Import button are correctly placed below this step]
5.0 Record Test Case
This step captures browser activity and translates it into a test script using JMeter's built-in proxy recorder.
Steps:
a. Return to the HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder in JMeter and click Start.
b. In the browser, perform the desired user journey (e.g., log in, browse pages, log out). JMeter will capture these actions as HTTP requests.
c. JMeter will log each HTTP request under the Recording Controller in your test plan tree.
d. Once done, click the Stop button in the HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder.
Captured requests are now saved in your test plan for review and refinement.
6.0 Refine the Test Case
Refining ensures your recorded test script is optimized, clean, and readable.
Steps:
a. After recording, review the generated test plan.
b. Inspect all recorded requests under the Recording Controller.
c. Remove unnecessary or irrelevant requests (e.g., images, scripts, analytics calls).
d. Add a View Results Tree listener to your Thread Group to monitor test output.
e. Save the test plan as a .jmx file (File → Save Test Plan As…).




3.0 Configure Request Filtering (Optional but Recommended)
To reduce unnecessary noise in your recorded scripts (such as calls to images, fonts, and stylesheets), configure Request Filtering in the HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder:
3.1 Switch to Request Filtering Tab
After adding the HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder, click on the "Request Filtering" tab.
3.2 Apply Filters Based on URL Pattern
In the “Include” section, add domain filters for the application you're testing.
Example (for FEMA URLs):
.*https://tdl\.(eservices|isource)\.fema\.gov.*
If unsure how many subdomains are involved, use a wildcard like:
.*fema\.gov.*
3.3 Add Exclusion Patterns
To filter out static files (e.g., images, fonts, JavaScript, CSS), use the “Exclude” section. Add the following pattern:
.*\.(png|jpg|jpeg|gif|bmp|ico|css|js|woff|woff2|svg|ttf|eot)*
You can also click "Add Suggested Excludes" to auto-populate these filters.
